## Login services

`hindsight-db.service` owns persistent VectorChord PostgreSQL.
`hindsight.service` owns the API/dashboard and requires the database unit to
finish its readiness check first. Both are enabled at user login; lingering is
not enabled. Both check the running system Docker daemon and its API before
starting, retrying every 15 seconds when unavailable. User units cannot declare
cross-manager `Requires=docker.service`; the existing system daemon is already
enabled and was not reconfigured.

The API, dashboard and PostgreSQL bind directly to IPv4 loopback using Docker
host networking. The former socket proxies are retired; Docker port publishing
is neither needed nor used.

```sh
systemctl --user status hindsight-db.service hindsight.service
systemctl --user restart hindsight.service
systemctl --user stop hindsight.service
systemctl --user stop hindsight-db.service
systemctl --user start hindsight.service
journalctl --user -u hindsight.service -u hindsight-db.service -n 100
```

Stopping Hindsight leaves the database running. Stopping the required database
also stops Hindsight. Starting Hindsight starts its required database. Data is
never deleted by these operations. Use PostgreSQL backup tools, or stop the
database before taking a filesystem copy.

