## Qwen host networking

The three existing EasyLlama containers (llama-swap, Qwen chat and embeddings)
also use host networking. Their owner remains `/mnt/LIBRARY/llamacpp/run.sh`;
the launcher now supports `docker.network_mode`, selected as `host` in its
local `config.json`. Restarting through that launcher preserves this choice.
The proxy binds to `127.0.0.1:8080`, model ports to 9000/9001 and lifecycle
ports to 9002/9003, all on loopback. Images, GPU allocations, resource limits
and model caches were preserved. Private rollback copies are under
`~/.local/state/hindsight/qwen-host-network-*/`. Unrelated containers were
not modified. 9router remains its existing host process on port 20128.

For the dashboard, `HINDSIGHT_CP_HOSTNAME=localhost` and
`NODE_OPTIONS=--dns-result-order=ipv4first` keep its listener on IPv4 loopback
without the shipped locale middleware redirect loop seen with a numeric
hostname. A temporary loopback probe reproduced and verified this fix.

`host-network-verification.json` records topology, health, 30 passing existing
EasyLlama unit tests, and successful synthetic chat/4096-dimensional embedding
requests from Hindsight through 9router, in separate probe rounds. Startup
returned chat 503 with embeddings 200; a later probe returned chat 200 but
timed out on embeddings after 30 seconds. Those were pre-fix observations;
see the swap reliability section for the current settings and validation.
Network success does not mean every queued import has finished.

## Swap reliability

Qwen chat and embeddings share one GPU through llama-swap. Three settings
previously combined into failures: the four-request admission limit counted
waiting requests, 9router allowed only 60 seconds for upstream headers, and the
Qwen template defaulted to extra-high reasoning during structured extraction.

- EasyLlama's live and example Qwen profiles now set `concurrencyLimit: 0` for
  both models. Requests queue instead of immediately receiving 429; backend
  `--parallel 4` and the mutually exclusive model group are unchanged. This is
  unbounded admission, not unbounded inference: keep client workloads bounded.
- `~/.config/9router/runtime.env` sets fetch-connect, first-chunk and stall
  deadlines to 600 seconds. `~/.local/bin/9router-local` loads this file and
  starts the installed CLI without modifying its package. Desktop login uses
  that launcher; use `9router-local --tray --skip-update` for manual startup.
- Hindsight uses one worker with no reserved consolidation slot, one LLM call
  per scope, one retain chunk per LLM call, and one embedding batch request with
  at most 16 items. LLM timeout is 900 seconds; retry backoff starts at 5 seconds
  and caps at 60 seconds. These conservative settings favor progress over bulk
  throughput on a swapping GPU; monitor before increasing concurrency.
- Only retain calls disable thinking, using
  `HINDSIGHT_API_RETAIN_LLM_REASONING_EFFORT=none` and
  `HINDSIGHT_API_RETAIN_LLM_EXTRA_BODY={"chat_template_kwargs":{"enable_thinking":false}}`.
  Other Qwen clients and Hindsight reflect/consolidation reasoning are unchanged.

Private backups and configuration/retry receipts are under
`~/.local/state/hindsight/swap-reliability-*/`; the latest validation is recorded
in `~/.local/state/hindsight/swap-reliability-verification.json`. Earlier probe
receipts are historical, not the current health result. Failed transport and
interrupted-recovery operations are resumed using Hindsight's official retry
endpoint with their existing operation IDs, never by reuploading the corpus.
Queue recovery does not mean corpus processing has finished.

Relevant upstream behavior: [llama-swap v251 configuration](https://github.com/mostlygeek/llama-swap/blob/v251/config.example.yaml).
